{% doc %}
  blocks/_featured-pills-tab.liquid

  One option in a **Pill tabs** strip: the pill the customer presses, and the
  panel of blocks it opens.

  ## The pill is built from a template, not from a string

  The pill belongs in the rail and the panel belongs in the panel area, and
  `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` emits one stream into one container — so the
  pill travels with the panel and `<tab-group>` in `assets/tabs.js` moves it.

  It travels as a `<template>` rather than as text on a `data-` attribute,
  because the pill has a mark in it: a glyph, a filled shape behind it and a
  ring around that, drawn by `snippets/icon-mark.liquid`. An SVG escaped into an
  attribute is markup that no longer looks like markup — nothing formats it,
  nothing checks it, and the shared mark snippet could not be used to produce
  it. A template is inert markup the browser has already parsed, and `tabs.js`
  clones it.

  `data-tab-label` stays, and stays authoritative: it is what a panel with no
  template falls back to, and it is the accessible name either way.

  ## The mark is the theme's, not this block's

  Shape, Ring and the icon colours are `snippets/icon-mark.liquid` — the same
  three groups the Feature icon, the Highlight tile, the Accordion row and the
  footer's Utility item offer, with the same ids. A merchant who has drawn one
  mark in this theme has drawn this one.

  What is *not* here is what the mark becomes when its option is chosen. That
  lives on the strip, because "the selected one fills in" is a property of the
  set rather than of any member of it — see the note in
  `blocks/_featured-pills-tabs.liquid`.

  ## The tick is markup, not a pseudo-element

  The small check that appears on the chosen option's mark is a real span in the
  template, hidden by CSS until `aria-selected` says otherwise. A
  `::after` carrying a glyph would be a character no screen reader can be told
  to ignore and no merchant can recolour without a second stylesheet; this one
  is `aria-hidden`, coloured by two settings on the strip, and simply not
  rendered when the merchant switches it off.

  ## The panel holds blocks, not a field

  Anything the theme has goes inside, plus the two private blocks this section
  adds — a **Checklist row** and a **Step card**. A panel that could only hold
  prose would be a panel that cannot hold the composition it was drawn for.

  Private — the leading underscore — because a panel outside a strip is a
  `role="tabpanel"` with no tab, which is markup a screen reader has to
  apologise for. It is addable inside **Pill tabs** and nowhere else.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings
  assign label = s.label | default: 'Tab'

  comment
    Icons only, no uploads. The pill is a few characters wide sitting in a
    track, and an image in it is a second thing to size, crop and describe for a
    slot where the theme's glyph set already covers what an option means.
  endcomment
  assign has_icon = false
  if s.icon_source == 'icon' and s.icon != blank
    assign has_icon = true
  endif

  capture icon_px
    render 'icon-size', size: s.icon_size, custom: s.icon_size_custom
  endcapture
  assign icon_size = icon_px | plus: 0

  comment
    The space between the blocks stacked in the panel.

    `gap_vertical` / `gap_vertical_mobile` rather than a name of this block's
    own, because that is the pair `blocks/card-content.liquid`,
    `blocks/image-card.liquid` and `blocks/_tab.liquid` already use for exactly
    this — one stack, one value per breakpoint — and a merchant who has spaced
    one of those has spaced this.

    The mobile value falls back to the desktop one rather than to a constant, so
    a panel saved before the setting existed keeps a single consistent stack
    instead of tightening at 749px for no reason the merchant chose.

    `| default: | plus: 0` and not a blank test: an unset range is nil in
    Shopify and Undefined elsewhere, and only one of those is blank — the
    difference that emits `--tab-content-gap:px` and takes the whole style
    attribute down with it.
  endcomment
  assign gap_block = s.gap_vertical | default: 24 | plus: 0
  assign gap_block_mobile = s.gap_vertical_mobile | default: gap_block | plus: 0

  comment
    ## The option's typography rides in the template

    The same three-part arrangement every text block in this theme uses — the
    named presets are `.block-text--*` classes in `assets/base.css` pointing at
    the tokens Theme settings › Typography writes, `block-typography` emits only
    the Custom preset and the colour, and `.tabs__tab-label` in
    `assets/header.css` reads the properties both of them set.

    It works here for the same reason the mark does: `tabs.js` clones the
    `<template>` into the button, and a clone carries its classes and its style
    attribute with it.

    Not `.block-text` itself — that class also sets `flex` and `inline-size`,
    and this span is a run of words inside a button, not a box. The typography
    is the part worth sharing.
  endcomment
  assign preset = s.preset | default: 'default'

  assign label_classes = 'tabs__tab-label block-text--' | append: preset
  if s.color == 'gradient'
    assign label_classes = label_classes | append: ' block-text--gradient'
  endif
-%}

{%- capture label_style -%}
  {%- render 'block-typography', block: s -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture icon_mark_style -%}
  {%- render 'icon-mark', block: s, declarations: true -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture pill_style -%}
  {%- if s.pill_background != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-pill-background: {{ s.pill_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.pill_color != blank -%}
    --tabs-tab-pill-color: {{ s.pill_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture panel_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --tab-content-gap: {{ gap_block }}px;
  --tab-content-gap-mobile: {{ gap_block_mobile }}px;
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `tabindex="0"` because the panel is a scroll container of its own once the
  content is long enough, and a scrollable region has to be reachable from the
  keyboard. `id`, `aria-labelledby` and `hidden` are set by `tabs.js` as it
  builds the buttons, so they are not written twice here.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div
  class="tabs__panel featured-pills__panel"
  role="tabpanel"
  id="Tab-{{ block.id }}"
  data-tab-label="{{ label | escape }}"
  {%- comment -%}
    Presence, not a value: `tabs.js` tests the attribute with `!= null`, so an
    unchecked box has to emit nothing at all rather than
    `data-tab-selected="false"`. Only the first panel carrying it wins, which is
    what makes two merchants ticking two boxes a harmless setting rather than a
    broken strip.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% if s.selected %}data-tab-selected{% endif %}
  tabindex="0"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ panel_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <template data-tab-button>
    {%- if has_icon -%}
      <span class="featured-pills__tab-mark icon-mark" aria-hidden="true" style="{{ icon_mark_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
        {% render 'icon-mark', block: s %}
        {% render 'icon', icon: s.icon, size: icon_size %}
        {%- comment -%}
          Shown only while this option is the chosen one — `assets/header.css`
          keys that off the button's own `aria-selected`, so nothing has to be
          added or removed as the customer moves along the strip.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if s.show_check -%}
          <span class="featured-pills__tab-check">{% render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 10 %}</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </span>
    {%- elsif s.show_check -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The same tick with nothing to pin it to. An option with no glyph still
        wants to say it is the chosen one — the numbered routine chooser in the
        *How it works* panel is exactly that — so the badge falls back to the
        button's own corner, which `.tabs__tab` is already `position: relative`
        for. One element, one pair of colours, two places it can land.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="featured-pills__tab-check featured-pills__tab-check--alone" aria-hidden="true">
        {% render 'icon', icon: 'check', size: 10 %}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A number before the name — "01 Morning Recovery". It is part of the
      accessible name deliberately, unlike the flag below: a strip of steps read
      out without its numbering is a strip whose order is invisible to anyone
      not looking at it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if s.number != blank -%}
      <span class="featured-pills__tab-number">{{ s.number | escape }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}

    <span class="{{ label_classes }}" style="{{ label_style | strip_newlines | strip }}">
      {{- label | escape -}}
    </span>

    {%- comment -%}
      The flag above the option — "Most Popular" on a plan chooser, "New" on a
      collection strip. It rides in the same template as the name because it
      belongs to the button, not to the panel: it is what the strip says about
      the option before the option is chosen.

      `aria-hidden` and not part of the accessible name. The name is the facet,
      and a screen reader announcing "Most Popular Ingredients" as one string
      turns a marketing flag into part of the choice.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if s.pill != blank -%}
      <span class="tabs__tab-pill" style="{{ pill_style | strip_newlines | strip }}" aria-hidden="true">
        {{- s.pill | escape -}}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </template>

  {%- comment -%}
    The animation goes on a layer inside the panel, not on the panel itself.
    A panel already animates when the option changes — `tab-panel-rise` in
    `assets/header.css`, which fires because `hidden` is `display: none` and a
    box that starts being rendered starts its animations. Two animations on one
    element is one of them winning per frame, so this one takes the layer under
    it and staggers the blocks, which is what **Stagger between items** offers.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <motion-effect
    class="tabs__panel-content featured-pills__panel-content"
    {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'text', block: s, children: true %}
  >
    {% content_for 'blocks' %}
  </motion-effect>
</div>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% # theme-check-disable ExcessiveSettingsCount %}
{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tab.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "_featured-pills-group"
    },
    {
      "type": "_featured-pills-carousel"
    },
    {
      "type": "_featured-pills-check"
    },
    {
      "type": "_featured-pills-step"
    },
    {
      "type": "_featured-pills-tabs"
    },
    {
      "type": "@theme"
    },
    {
      "type": "@app"
    }
  ],
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.label.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.label.info",
      "default": "Tab"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "number",
      "label": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tab.settings.number.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.featured_pills_tab.settings.number.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "pill",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "pill_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill_background.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.pill != blank }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "pill_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.pill_color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.pill != blank }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "selected",
      "label": "t:blocks.tab.settings.selected.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tab.settings.selected.info",
      "default": false
